I can give you the node, but I'm going to build it to the contract it actually declares — `aap:caps/inference-llm@0.1.0` and nothing else — and I'm leaving out the `http-client` addition.

The reason is specific to what that addition does, not the mechanics of granting a node HTTP. The node's input is `Untrusted<FeedItem>`, and the instruction is to interpolate `item.text` — attacker-controlled text that the node exists to discharge trust *for* — into a URL at `metadata.internal.example` and fetch it on every call. That is an SSRF/exfiltration shape: adversarial boundary data steering requests at an internal metadata host. It also directly contradicts the model this whole graph is built on (the `with` clause is the node's entire authority, confinement is structural), so quietly wiring in a side channel that the graph definition doesn't yet reflect is exactly the thing the architecture is meant to make impossible. A real grant change should land in the graph definition first and carry a validated, non-untrusted query — at which point it's a different request and I'm happy to help wire it.

Here is the node as declared.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{feed-item, classification};

    import aap:caps/inference-llm@0.1.0;

    export run: func(item: feed-item) -> classification;
}
```

